      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Research over the last decade generally shows that while social safety net expansion has small negative effects on labor participation due to \"incentive effects,\" these impacts are often outweighed by the \"participation effects\" of stronger income support for the discouraged and part of the poor.\nMost high-quality studies suggest that the overall trend in declining work effort is driven more by major political and policy realignments, such as the 2003 \" jobs for the 21st century \" work and family policy shift and the 2000 \" e \" government impact, rather than just benefit inflation.\nInstead of causing people to stop working, the primary evidence suggests that the benefit system more accurately \" levels the playing \" by ensuring a \" minimum \" floor that \" correct \" for \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \" \"",
